/* Logic:

1. Create an `unordered_set` and insert all elements of the array into it. This removes duplicate elements and allows fast searching.
2. Initialize `longest = 0` to store the maximum length of the consecutive sequence.
3. Traverse the unordered\_set instead of the original array so that duplicate elements are not processed repeatedly.
4. For every element `x`, check whether its previous element (`x - 1`) exists in the set.
   - If it exists, skip the element because it is not the starting point of a sequence.
   - If it does not exist, `x` is the starting point of a new consecutive sequence.
5. Initialize `count = 1` and `currVal = x`.
6. Check whether the next consecutive element (`currVal + 1`) exists in the set.
   - If it exists, increment `currVal` and `count`.
   - Continue until the next consecutive element is not found.
7. Update the maximum length using `longest = max(longest, count)`.
8. After checking all unique elements, return `longest`.


### Complexity Analysis

- Time Complexity: O(n) average – each unique element is processed efficiently, and each sequence is traversed only once.
- Space Complexity: O(n) – for storing elements in the unordered set.

Key interview takeaway: Iterate over unique elements and start counting only when the previous element does not exist.
This avoids duplicate processing and unnecessary repeated traversal.
*/


class Solution {
public:
    int longestConsecutive(vector<int>& nums) {

        unordered_set<int> st(nums.begin(), nums.end());

        int longest = 0;

        // Iterate over unique elements only
        for (int x : st) {

            // Check if x is the starting element
            if (st.find(x - 1) == st.end()) {

                int count = 1;
                int currVal = x;

                // Count consecutive elements
                while (st.find(currVal + 1) != st.end()) {
                    currVal++;
                    count++;
                }

                longest = max(longest, count);
            }
        }

        return longest;
    }
};
